1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,222
A mango leaf, dark on top and pale below.

2
00:00:03,222 --> 00:00:05,797
A grass blade, the same on both sides.

3
00:00:05,797 --> 00:00:09,333
Grass on the playground,
rolled up in the hot afternoon.

4
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,533
Three leaves, three puzzles.

5
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:14,137
The answers are all
hidden inside the leaf.

6
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:18,536
Diya picked a grass blade that had
curled up in the sun and asked: did

7
00:00:18,536 --> 00:00:19,969
it roll up on purpose?

8
00:00:20,319 --> 00:00:21,219
It did.

9
00:00:20,937 --> 00:00:25,074
And to see how, we have to slice a
leaf and look at it from the side.

10
00:00:25,574 --> 00:00:30,098
By the end, you will read a dicot
leaf and a monocot leaf section, and

11
00:00:30,098 --> 00:00:32,657
watch a grass leaf curl to save water.

12
00:00:33,307 --> 00:00:36,360
First, every leaf section
has three main parts.

13
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:41,509
The epidermis: the skin, on the upper
and the lower surface, with a cuticle.

14
00:00:41,959 --> 00:00:45,625
The mesophyll: the green filling with
chloroplasts, where photosynthesis

15
00:00:45,625 --> 00:00:46,525
happens.

16
00:00:46,668 --> 00:00:51,223
And the vascular system: the vascular
bundles in the veins and the midrib.

17
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:53,725
Let's zoom into a mango leaf.

18
00:00:53,725 --> 00:00:57,651
It is dorsiventral, which means
its top and bottom are different.

19
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:00,779
The upper skin is the adaxial epidermis.

20
00:01:00,779 --> 00:01:03,557
The lower skin is the abaxial epidermis.

21
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:06,283
The lower one usually has more stomata.

22
00:01:06,283 --> 00:01:08,104
The upper may have none.

23
00:01:08,604 --> 00:01:13,795
Just below the upper skin stands the
palisade parenchyma: long cells, standing

24
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:17,249
upright, side by side,
like pencils in a box.

25
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:22,883
Below them lies the spongy parenchyma:
round or oval cells, loosely packed,

26
00:01:22,883 --> 00:01:25,397
with large air spaces between them.

27
00:01:25,897 --> 00:01:29,418
Both layers have chloroplasts, and
both make food by photosynthesis.

28
00:01:29,918 --> 00:01:34,433
The veins carry vascular bundles,
wrapped in thick-walled bundle sheath

29
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:35,333
cells.

30
00:01:35,082 --> 00:01:38,811
In a net of veins, the
bundles come in many sizes.

31
00:01:39,461 --> 00:01:40,588
Now a grass leaf.

32
00:01:40,588 --> 00:01:43,565
It is isobilateral, which
means both sides are alike.

33
00:01:43,565 --> 00:01:45,171
Watch it handle a hot day.

34
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:47,722
Its stomata are on both surfaces.

35
00:01:47,722 --> 00:01:51,425
Its mesophyll is not split into
palisade and spongy layers.

36
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:56,647
Along the veins, some upper epidermal
cells are large, empty and colourless.

37
00:01:56,647 --> 00:01:58,257
These are bulliform cells.

38
00:01:59,007 --> 00:02:02,986
When there is plenty of water, the
bulliform cells are full and firm.

39
00:02:02,986 --> 00:02:04,776
The leaf stays flat and open.

40
00:02:05,526 --> 00:02:09,903
On a hot day with little water, the
bulliform cells lose water and go limp.

41
00:02:10,653 --> 00:02:12,554
Then the leaf curls inward.

42
00:02:12,554 --> 00:02:16,848
Less of its surface is open to
the air, so less water is lost.

43
00:02:17,598 --> 00:02:21,110
Now the pair to tell apart: the
dorsiventral leaf and the isobilateral

44
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:22,010
leaf.

45
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:26,394
Both have an epidermis with a cuticle,
a green mesophyll, and vascular

46
00:02:26,394 --> 00:02:27,294
bundles.

47
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:32,049
But a dorsiventral leaf has palisade
and spongy layers, and more stomata

48
00:02:32,049 --> 00:02:32,949
below.

49
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:37,516
An isobilateral leaf has one kind of
mesophyll, and stomata on both sides.

50
00:02:38,166 --> 00:02:39,066
Your turn.

51
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:42,648
A leaf section shows stomata on both
surfaces, and no palisade layer.

52
00:02:42,648 --> 00:02:44,336
Dorsiventral, or isobilateral?

53
00:02:44,336 --> 00:02:45,401
Pause and decide.

54
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:49,501
Isobilateral!

55
00:02:49,386 --> 00:02:53,599
Like a grass leaf: stomata on both
sides, and a mesophyll that is not split.

56
00:02:54,249 --> 00:02:55,149
Remember this.

57
00:02:55,149 --> 00:02:57,252
Dorsiventral: two kinds of mesophyll.

58
00:02:57,252 --> 00:03:00,178
Isobilateral: one kind,
with stomata on both sides.

59
00:03:00,778 --> 00:03:02,358
Now, a worked example.

60
00:03:02,358 --> 00:03:05,441
Let's sort four clues into
the right kind of leaf.

61
00:03:05,791 --> 00:03:08,705
Palisade cells below the upper epidermis.

62
00:03:08,705 --> 00:03:10,841
That is a dorsiventral leaf.

63
00:03:11,291 --> 00:03:12,583
Stomata on both surfaces.

64
00:03:12,583 --> 00:03:14,069
That is an isobilateral leaf.

65
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:16,163
Bulliform cells along the veins.

66
00:03:16,163 --> 00:03:17,692
That is an isobilateral leaf.

67
00:03:18,142 --> 00:03:21,212
A net of veins, with
bundles of many sizes.

68
00:03:21,212 --> 00:03:23,180
That is a dorsiventral leaf.

69
00:03:23,830 --> 00:03:25,295
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:25,645 --> 00:03:28,313
Many students think bulliform cells
are packed with chloroplasts, like

71
00:03:28,313 --> 00:03:29,213
the mesophyll.

72
00:03:29,385 --> 00:03:32,494
But bulliform cells are
large, empty and colourless.

73
00:03:32,494 --> 00:03:35,585
Their job is to curl the
leaf, not to make food.

74
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:37,621
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:41,912
As statements and diagram labels: which
surface has more stomata, palisade

76
00:03:41,912 --> 00:03:44,630
versus spongy, and what
bulliform cells do.

77
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:46,361
Exam level now.

78
00:03:46,361 --> 00:03:48,577
Read the assertion and the reason.

79
00:03:48,577 --> 00:03:49,757
Pause and solve.

80
00:03:55,157 --> 00:03:56,227
The answer is A.

81
00:03:56,227 --> 00:03:59,166
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

82
00:03:59,666 --> 00:04:04,235
Bulliform cells are large, empty cells
on the upper side, along the veins.

83
00:04:04,735 --> 00:04:09,623
When they go limp, or flaccid, the
leaf curls inward and loses less water.

84
00:04:10,223 --> 00:04:11,763
Here is your recap.

85
00:04:11,763 --> 00:04:13,346
Screenshot this card.

86
00:04:16,546 --> 00:04:18,732
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

87
00:04:19,132 --> 00:04:20,032
One.

88
00:04:19,606 --> 00:04:22,854
Which surface of a dorsiventral
leaf has more stomata?

89
00:04:23,754 --> 00:04:24,654
Two.

90
00:04:24,210 --> 00:04:27,142
How are palisade cells
different from spongy cells?

91
00:04:28,042 --> 00:04:28,942
Three.

92
00:04:28,486 --> 00:04:30,582
What do bulliform cells
do in water stress?

93
00:04:31,682 --> 00:04:36,015
Next time: how does a thin sapling
become a thick trunk with rings?

94
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:38,153
We find out in secondary growth.
